WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.155
Consider an inbox checked once every sixty
seconds. A message arriving immediately after a

00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:10.252
check can remain invisible for almost a minute.
Checking every second improves freshness,

00:00:10.252 --> 00:00:15.293
but one thousand idle browsers then generate
about one thousand requests per second even

00:00:15.293 --> 00:00:16.496
when no mail arrives.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:21.495
The interval trades resource use against update
delay. Long polling changes the exchange:

00:00:21.495 --> 00:00:26.489
the browser asks for an update and the server
leaves that request pending until a message

00:00:26.489 --> 00:00:31.203
exists or a timeout occurs. The browser
immediately starts another request after the

00:00:31.203 --> 00:00:31.708
response.

00:00:31.708 --> 00:00:36.801
Draw the long quiet interval as a pending
request, not as repeated empty responses. This

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:41.952
explains both the low update latency and the
need to manage many outstanding connections.

00:00:41.952 --> 00:00:47.185
A WebSocket establishes a persistent channel
that supports messages in both directions. For

00:00:47.185 --> 00:00:52.476
an active collaboration page, cursor updates can
travel to the server while document changes

00:00:52.476 --> 00:00:56.904
travel back without opening a new application-
level request for every update.

00:00:56.952 --> 00:01:02.157
Long polling instead completes an HTTP response
and opens the next request. Neither

00:01:02.157 --> 00:01:07.424
transport removes failure: a laptop sleeps, a
proxy closes an idle path, or a server

00:01:07.424 --> 00:01:13.005
restarts. When reconnecting, the client should
identify what it last received and recover

00:01:13.005 --> 00:01:16.266
missing state according to the application
protocol.

00:01:16.452 --> 00:01:21.533
Otherwise a successful new connection can hide
an incomplete view. Choose server-sent events

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:26.282
when a one-way server stream fits; choose a
bidirectional channel when the interaction

00:01:26.282 --> 00:01:27.441
actually requires it.

00:01:27.452 --> 00:01:32.346
Suppose a browser's live connection terminates
at notification worker B. A ride update

00:01:32.346 --> 00:01:37.467
happens on application worker A. Sending the
update to an arbitrary notification worker is

00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:42.645
insufficient because only B owns the existing
socket. A routing registry therefore maps the

00:01:42.645 --> 00:01:45.888
user's active session to B and its connection
identifier.

00:01:45.952 --> 00:01:51.022
Worker A sends a logical notification to the
notification service; the service looks up the

00:01:51.022 --> 00:01:56.147
destination and forwards to B. The registry is
the bridge between a stable business identity

00:01:56.147 --> 00:02:01.273
and a temporary physical route. Adding a load
balancer helps distribute new connections, but

00:02:01.273 --> 00:02:05.061
does not make all established connections
available on every worker.

00:02:05.061 --> 00:02:08.904
Trace ownership explicitly when drawing a
distributed push design.

00:02:09.061 --> 00:02:14.550
Now let the user open two browser tabs and also
register a phone. One logical event can map

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:20.099
to three destinations, possibly using two
different delivery mechanisms. Closing a tab
makes

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:24.925
one route obsolete; restarting its worker can
make many routes obsolete at once.

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:29.997
A lookup can race with a disconnect, so a
routing result is not a guarantee of delivery.

00:02:29.997 --> 00:02:34.878
Remove invalid destinations and use connection
lifetimes or leases to prevent abandoned

00:02:34.878 --> 00:02:39.646
records accumulating. Decide whether every
destination needs the event or whether one

00:02:39.646 --> 00:02:41.273
current device is sufficient.

00:02:41.273 --> 00:02:46.142
Persist the business event separately from this
transient registry. After a failure, the

00:02:46.142 --> 00:02:50.845
user can reconnect and retrieve the current ride
even if none of the old routes still

00:02:50.845 --> 00:02:51.232
exists.
